{"text": "Apple turned the smallest share of fiscal 2025 revenue into free cash flow: 23.7% [f_45a1bbe371f1], versus 25.4% [f_7a222b15e152] for Microsoft and 46.6% [f_e8440ce44e3a] for NVIDIA.

On the portfolio side, I cannot state the post-sale Technology sector weight or which concentration checks would loosen or tighten from the held book rows alone. The desk’s current book rows show Technology at 35.3% [f_2645c6724868], gross exposure at 100.0% [f_86fe38f80e5f], AAPL issuer concentration at 15.2% [f_6666630333f6], MSFT at 16.0% [f_503b04dfc955], NVDA at 4.06% [f_e40bfeb5384f], Technology sector concentration at 35.3% [f_a34bf1e057b4], daily_loss at -0.38% [f_4abff85af07c], and rolling_volatility_30d at 12.5% [f_37bbead09378]. Those are the only checked figures I can rely on here.

What I can say is directional only: selling AAPL entirely would remove a Technology holding from the book, so the Technology sector’s weight would fall, and the AAPL issuer-concentration check would loosen. I do not have a checked post-trade book row to say whether any other check tightens or whether the Technology sector or gross exposure moves to a new warning tier."}

{"text": "Among the equity holdings, Apple is the one furthest below its 52-week high, at -3.89% [f_d06c9c2cf3a3].

